---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import { sitePath } from "../lib/site-path.mjs";
---

<SiteLayout title="sum — Many agents. One finished task." foot="home">
  <main>
    <div class="hero">
      <div class="main">
        <h1 class="display">Many agents.<br /><em>One finished task.</em></h1>
        <p class="lede">Working instructions for your usual coding agent in an existing HC-backed Herdr terminal. Implement directly, start useful helpers, receive native reports, and finish with checked results in the same conversation.</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="btn" href={sitePath("install")}>Start</a>
          <a class="btn outline" href={sitePath("architecture")}>How it works</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="rail">
        <div><div class="lbl">Your conversation</div>The agent you launch is the lead. It can write code and ask a bounded helper for independent work.</div>
        <div><div class="lbl">Process owner</div>Native HC owns terminals and communication. Git provides checkout isolation.</div>
        <div><div class="lbl">Written checkpoint</div>A small task note and separate helper reports retain decisions, evidence, and the next action.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="band">
      <div class="feature"><div class="n">01</div><div class="t">One writer per checkout</div><p>Use separate Git worktrees for concurrent writers. Review and checks inspect a stable candidate. These are working conventions, not enforced locks.</p></div>
      <div class="feature"><div class="n">02</div><div class="t">Four short procedures</div><p style="margin-bottom:14px">Read the procedure for the current action. <a href={sitePath("skills")}>Skills →</a></p><div class="chips"><span class="chip">Work</span><span class="chip">Review</span><span class="chip">Finish</span><span class="chip">Resume</span></div></div>
      <div class="feature"><div class="n">03</div><div class="t">Evidence for the candidate</div><p>Run the project’s required checks, resolve independent findings, and account for results against the final candidate. <a href={sitePath("verification")}>Verification →</a></p></div>
      <div class="feature"><div class="n">04</div><div class="t">The ordinary native lead</div><p>Start the supported Herdr app independently and launch your usual interactive agent in its HC-backed terminal. Keep your account, model, and permissions.</p></div>
      <div class="feature"><div class="n">05</div><div class="t">Reports arrive natively</div><p>HC reports command exits. Interactive helpers save reports and explicitly send results to their immediate parent, then remain available for follow-up. <a href={sitePath("herdr")}>Native HC →</a></p></div>
      <div class="feature"><div class="n">06</div><div class="t">Portable verification</div><p>A project-root <code>VERIFY.md</code> describes project checks. Optional development tools remain independent of ordinary startup.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="band">
      <div class="split">
        <div class="eyebrow">Quick start</div>
        <div class="codeblock"><div>git clone https://github.com/douglasjarquin/sum.git</div><div>cd /path/to/sum</div><div>codex --no-daemon</div></div>
        <p style="margin:18px 0 0;font-size:12px">Run these from an existing supported HC-backed Herdr terminal. Give the agent your task. Sum requires no executable, build, setup, initialization, or service.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="split">
        <div class="eyebrow">How it works</div>
        <div class="kv flow" style="grid-template-columns:130px 1fr"><div class="k">you</div><div class="v">approved requests and decisions ↓</div><div class="k" style="color:var(--accent)">lead</div><div class="v">direct work, bounded briefs, native launch ↓</div><div class="k">helper</div><div class="v">saved report and explicit native send ↑</div></div>
        <p style="margin:18px 0 0;font-size:12px">Authenticated native acceptance remains unrun. Observed versions do not imply qualification. Read the <a href={sitePath("herdr")}>native prerequisites and limits</a> before delegation.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</SiteLayout>
